<h1>User journeys and use cases</h1>
<p class="lede">Who uses the app, what they want to achieve, and the path they take. Engineers call this "use cases"; it is the bridge between the product and the design.</p>

<h2>The people (actors)</h2>
<table>
  <tr><th>Actor</th><th>Wants to…</th></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Developer</strong></td><td>see every bug assigned to them, notice changes immediately, avoid missing a reopened bug</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Tech lead / QA lead</strong></td><td>see all bugs of an epic, their age and status, who holds them, and how the backlog trends</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Visitor</strong></td><td>try the app with sample data before connecting anything</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Operator</strong> (you, hosting the Cloud edition)</td><td>deploy, monitor, keep costs at zero, respond to privacy duties</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Atlassian</strong> (a system, not a person)</td><td>authenticate users, serve Jira data, ask for personal-data reports</td></tr>
  <tr><td><strong>Cloudflare</strong> (a system)</td><td>run the code, store data, wake the monitor every minute</td></tr>
</table>

